-- title:
--   §2, p. 3 — A_ε(t) = εA(t/ε) is the G^ε-compensator of the thinned process N_ε
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a simple arrival counting process on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ with $\mathcal F$-compensator $A$ (predictable, $A(0)=0$, non-decreasing right-continuous paths, $N-A$ an $\mathcal F$-martingale), and assume $E\,N(t)<\infty$ for every $t\ge0$. Fix $0<\varepsilon\le1$ and let $\beta=(\beta_i)_{i\ge1}$ be iid Bernoulli($\varepsilon$), independent of $\mathcal F_\infty=\bigvee_{t}\mathcal F_t$.
--
--   Then the σ-algebras $\mathcal G^\varepsilon_t=\mathcal F_{t/\varepsilon}\vee\sigma(\beta_i:1\le i\le N(t/\varepsilon))$, $t\ge0$, form a filtration $\mathcal G^\varepsilon$, and $A_\varepsilon(t)=\varepsilon A(t/\varepsilon)$ is the $\mathcal G^\varepsilon$-compensator of $N_\varepsilon(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{N(t/\varepsilon)}\beta_i$: $A_\varepsilon$ is $\mathcal G^\varepsilon$-predictable with $A_\varepsilon(0)=0$ and non-decreasing right-continuous paths, and
--   $$E\big[N_\varepsilon(t+s)-A_\varepsilon(t+s)\,\big|\,\mathcal G^\varepsilon_t\big]=N_\varepsilon(t)-A_\varepsilon(t)\qquad(s,t\ge0).$$
--
--   This is the display on p. 3 and the sentence after it. It identifies the compensator of $N_\varepsilon$ to which the Poisson limit theorem is applied.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Two hypotheses are added to the page, both necessary. (i) The page assumes $\beta^\varepsilon$ independent of $N$ only, but its own step that replaces $\mathcal G^\varepsilon_t$ by $\mathcal F_{t/\varepsilon}$ needs independence from $\mathcal F_\infty$: with $\varepsilon=1/2$, a fair coin $U\in\mathcal F_0$ independent of $N$ and $\beta_1=U\oplus\mathbf 1\{N(1)\text{ even}\}$, the mark $\beta_1$ is Bernoulli(1/2) and independent of $N$, but $\mathcal G^\varepsilon_t$ then reveals the parity of $N(1)$ before time $1$ and $N_\varepsilon-A_\varepsilon$ is not a $\mathcal G^\varepsilon$-martingale. Independence is stated between the σ-algebra generated by the sequence $(\beta_1,\beta_2,\dots)$ (product σ-algebra on $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N}$) and $\bigvee_t\mathcal F_t$. (ii) "$M$ is a martingale" gives $E|N(t)-A(t)|<\infty$ but not $E\,N(t)<\infty$, which the integrability of $N_\varepsilon(t)$ needs; $E\,N(t)<\infty$ is assumed. The filtration is asserted to exist with $\mathcal G^\varepsilon_t$ as its value at $t$ (monotonicity and inclusion in $\mathcal F$ are part of the claim). The compensator is the predictable one, as in the definitions file.
-- source:
--   Zheng, Honnappa & Glynn, arXiv:1807.06805v1, p. 3, §2: the six-line display and "so that Aǫ is the Gǫ-compensator of Nǫ"

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RapidRatePoisson_WeakConv_Model

namespace RapidRatePoisson.WeakConv
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

theorem thinned_compensator {Ω : Type*} [m : MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (F : Filtration ℝ≥0 m) (N A : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ)
    (hN : ∀ ω, MartingaleHT.InfiniteServer.IsUnitJumpCountingPath (fun t => N t ω))
    (hA : IsCompensator F P N A) (hNint : ∀ t, Integrable (N t) P)
    (ε : ℝ≥0) (hε : 0 < ε) (hε1 : ε ≤ 1) (β : ℕ → Ω → Bool) (hβ : IsBernoulliSeq P ε β)
    (hβF : Indep (MeasurableSpace.comap (markSeq β) inferInstance) (⨆ t, F t) P) :
    ∃ G : Filtration ℝ≥0 m, (∀ t, G t = gSigma F N β ε t) ∧
      IsCompensator G P (thinned N β ε) (scaledComp A ε) := by sorry

end RapidRatePoisson.WeakConv
